---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { href } from '../../i18n/routes';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const page = 'labels' as const;
---
<Docs lang={lang} page={page}
  title="Labels & decorations"
  description="The thirteen labels, decorations, blocking precedence rules, and fifteen diagnostics that define how Conventional Comments are interpreted.">
<div class="dhead stack g12">
          <p class="crumb"><b>docs</b> / labels &amp; decorations</p>
          <h1>Labels &amp; decorations</h1>
          <p class="lede measure">Thirteen labels, three built-in decorations, and five precedence rules determine how a comment is interpreted — including whether it blocks a merge. This page is the reference for all of them.</p>
        </div>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>The grammar</h2>
          <p>Every validated comment starts with a prefix line. Anything after the first blank line is discussion. It is left entirely untouched by the parser.</p>
          <div class="anno">
            <pre class="line"><span class="tk-l">label</span> <span class="tk-d">[decorations]</span><span class="tk-c">:</span> subject

<span class="tk-k">[discussion]</span></pre>
            <div class="legend">
              <i style="color:var(--accent)">■ label — required, lowercase</i>
              <i>■ decorations — optional, comma-separated, inside parentheses</i>
              <i style="color:var(--block)">■ : — separates the prefix from the subject</i>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="small">Casing errors are corrected rather than rejected. For example, <code>Issue:</code> raises <code>W-CASE</code>, a warning with an automatic fix.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>The thirteen labels</h2>
          <p>Ten labels are enabled by default. Three more — <code>typo</code>, <code>polish</code>, and <code>quibble</code> — are available but disabled initially. They provide more specific alternatives to <code>todo</code>, <code>suggestion</code>, and <code>nitpick</code>, and repositories can enable them when useful.</p>
          <div class="scroller">
            <table class="t">
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Label</th><th>What it means</th><th>Blocking</th><th>Shortcut</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-praise)">🎉 praise</span></td><td class="d">Highlights something done well.</td><td class="m d">Depends on decoration</td><td class="m">?pr</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-nitpick)">🔍 nitpick</span></td><td class="d">A minor preference with nothing significant at stake.</td><td class="m"><span class="deco nb">Never</span></td><td class="m">?ni</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-suggestion)">💡 suggestion</span></td><td class="d">A reasoned proposal for improving something.</td><td class="m d">Depends on decoration</td><td class="m">?su</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-issue)">🔨 issue</span></td><td class="d">Identifies a problem. Ideally accompanied by a suggestion.</td><td class="m"><span class="deco b">By default</span></td><td class="m">?i · ?ib</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-todo)">📌 todo</span></td><td class="d">A small but necessary change.</td><td class="m"><span class="deco b">By default</span></td><td class="m">?to</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-question)">❓ question</span></td><td class="d">Asks for clarification about something uncertain.</td><td class="m d">Depends on decoration</td><td class="m">?que</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-thought)">💭 thought</span></td><td class="d">An idea that came up during the review, with no action requested.</td><td class="m"><span class="deco nb">Never</span></td><td class="m">?th</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-chore)">🧹 chore</span></td><td class="d">A side task that should be completed before acceptance.</td><td class="m"><span class="deco b">By default</span></td><td class="m">?ch</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-note)">📝 note</span></td><td class="d">Information worth calling out.</td><td class="m"><span class="deco nb">Never</span></td><td class="m">?no</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-decision)">🏁 decision</span></td><td class="d">Records the choice <em>not</em> to address a point and explains why.</td><td class="m"><span class="deco nb">Never</span></td><td class="m">?de</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m" style="opacity:.6"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-typo)">🔤 typo</span></td><td class="d">A typing mistake.</td><td class="m d">Disabled by default</td><td class="m">?ty</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m" style="opacity:.6"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-polish)">✨ polish</span></td><td class="d">A finishing improvement.</td><td class="m d">Disabled by default</td><td class="m">?po</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m" style="opacity:.6"><span class="pill" style="--lc:var(--l-quibble)">🪶 quibble</span></td><td class="d">A debatable detail.</td><td class="m d">Disabled by default</td><td class="m">?qui</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="small">Colours and icons are deliberately cosmetic. They are excluded from the enterprise floor. Changing them can never change a verdict or be locked down by an administrator.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2><code style="font-size:.9em">decision</code> — the label added by this toolkit</h2>
          <p><code>decision</code> is not part of the original Conventional Comments specification. The toolkit adds it because <code>enforce</code> needs a controlled way to resolve exceptional situations. Without one, a blocking thread could remain unresolved indefinitely simply because its author is unavailable.</p>
          <div class="split">
            <div class="callout">
              <span class="ct">it is recognized even where replies are not normally validated</span>
              <h4>The one label detected in an otherwise unvalidated reply</h4>
              <p>Thread replies are not validated by default (<code>validateReplies: false</code>). A reply whose resolved label is <code>decision</code> is still recognized. Otherwise, this mechanism would only work in repositories that had independently enabled validation for replies.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="callout">
              <span class="ct">and a decision must actually explain the decision</span>
              <h4><code>E-DECISION-SUBJECT</code> is an error, not a warning</h4>
              <p>A <code>decision</code> reply must meet <code>minDecisionSubjectLength</code>, which defaults to 20 characters. That is the only subject-length threshold applied to <code>decision</code>. Resolving a blocking thread therefore requires an actual explanation. <code>wontfix</code> is not enough.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <pre class="code"><span class="tk-l">decision</span><span class="tk-c">:</span> out of scope for this PR, debt tracked in PROJ-142

Agreed with the platform team on 3 Sep: the cache rewrite lands in Q4
and this handler is deleted with it.</pre>
          <p class="small">Not everyone is allowed to record a <code>decision</code>. <code>resolverOverrideGroup</code> identifies the group whose members may override a resolution. Configuring that group is a prerequisite for <code>enforce</code>; leaving it empty disables both override mechanisms.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Decorations</h2>
          <p>Decorations qualify the meaning of a label. Three built-in decorations affect the blocking verdict. Custom decorations are also allowed by default: they can carry useful meaning for people without influencing whether the comment blocks.</p>
          <div class="scroller">
            <table class="t">
              <thead><tr><th>Decoration</th><th>Effect</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="deco b">blocking</span></td><td class="m" style="color:var(--block)">Blocking</td><td class="d">Must be resolved before the subject under review can be accepted.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="deco nb">non-blocking</span></td><td class="m" style="color:var(--pass)">Non-blocking</td><td class="d">Must not prevent acceptance.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="deco nb">if-minor</span></td><td class="m" style="color:var(--pass)">Non-blocking</td><td class="d">Address it only if the required change turns out to be trivial. The author decides.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m"><span class="deco">security</span> <span class="deco">test</span> <span class="deco">ux</span></td><td class="m d">None</td><td class="d">Custom decorations. They can be displayed, counted, and indexed, but never affect the verdict. Organizations can define their own.</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="small">An undecorated <code>suggestion</code> or <code>question</code> raises <code>W-MISSING-DECORATION</code>. Those are the two labels for which a reader genuinely cannot infer the blocking intent from the label alone.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Precedence — how blocking is determined</h2>
          <p>Five rules are evaluated in order. The first matching rule determines the result. There are no additional heuristics or hidden inputs.</p>
          <div class="scroller">
            <table class="t">
              <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Condition</th><th>Result</th><th>Diagnostic</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td class="m num">1</td><td class="d">The label is <code>alwaysNonBlocking</code>, but a decoration forces blocking.</td><td class="m">Use the label's own default.</td><td class="m" style="color:var(--block)">E-CONFLICT</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m num">2</td><td class="d">One decoration forces blocking while another forces non-blocking.</td><td class="m">Use the label's own default.</td><td class="m" style="color:var(--block)">E-CONFLICT</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m num">3</td><td class="d">A decoration forces blocking.</td><td class="m" style="color:var(--block)">Blocking</td><td class="m d">—</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m num">4</td><td class="d">A decoration forces non-blocking.</td><td class="m" style="color:var(--pass)">Non-blocking</td><td class="m d">—</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m num">5</td><td class="d">No decoration forces either result.</td><td class="m">Use the label's <code>blockingByDefault</code> value.</td><td class="m d">—</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <div class="callout">
            <span class="ct">why a conflicting comment still gets a verdict</span>
            <p style="margin:0">Rules 1 and 2 report an error, but they still produce a result. When a comment contradicts itself, the label's default is used as a deterministic fallback. Leaving the blocking state undefined would be worse: the system would be unable to determine whether the thread prevents acceptance, potentially leaving the pull request blocked with no actionable way to resolve the ambiguity.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Diagnostics</h2>
          <p>There are fifteen diagnostic codes. <strong>Errors</strong> make a comment non-compliant. They only affect merge eligibility when the repository configures <code>formatSeverity: "error"</code>. <strong>Warnings</strong> are reported and counted but never cause a failure by themselves. Every diagnostic can be assigned a different severity — or disabled entirely — per repository through <code>severities</code>.</p>
          <div class="split">
            <div class="scroller">
              <table class="t">
                <thead><tr><th style="color:var(--block)">Diagnostic</th><th>Raised when</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td class="m">E-NO-LABEL</td><td class="d">The comment has no prefix line.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">E-MALFORMED-PREFIX</td><td class="d">The prefix resembles valid syntax but cannot be parsed.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">E-UNKNOWN-LABEL</td><td class="d">The label is not enabled for this repository.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">E-EMPTY-SUBJECT</td><td class="d">A label and colon are present, but the subject is empty.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">E-DECORATION-SYNTAX</td><td class="d">Decoration parentheses or separators are malformed.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">E-UNKNOWN-DECORATION</td><td class="d">The decoration is unknown and custom decorations are disabled.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">E-CONFLICT</td><td class="d">Blocking precedence rule 1 or 2 applies.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">E-DECISION-SUBJECT</td><td class="d">A <code>decision</code> does not provide a sufficient explanation.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            <div class="scroller">
              <table class="t">
                <thead><tr><th style="color:var(--warn)">Diagnostic</th><th>Raised when</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td class="m">W-CASE</td><td class="d">The label uses the wrong casing. Includes an automatic fix.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">W-DECORATION-STYLE</td><td class="d">Decoration spacing or ordering is inconsistent. Includes an automatic fix.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">W-SUBJECT-TOO-SHORT</td><td class="d">The subject contains fewer than 5 characters.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">W-SUBJECT-TOO-LONG</td><td class="d">The subject exceeds 120 characters.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">W-MISSING-DECORATION</td><td class="d">A <code>suggestion</code> or <code>question</code> has no decoration.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">W-NO-DISCUSSION</td><td class="d">A blocking comment contains no discussion.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="m">W-NOT-BLOCKABLE</td><td class="d">A comment is marked as blocking in a context that cannot carry blocking state.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="callout acc">
            <span class="ct">calibration matters</span>
            <h4>A short subject is deliberately only a warning.</h4>
            <p>The threshold is five characters, and falling below it never produces an error. A short subject is not serious enough to justify blocking a workflow. Making minor issues punitive encourages people to work around the convention instead of using it. Enforcement works better when it reserves hard failures for things that genuinely matter.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <div class="pager">
          <a class="pgl" href={href('howitworks', 'en')}><span class="dir">← previous</span><span class="ttl">How it works</span></a>
          <a class="pgl" href={href('adoption', 'en')} style="text-align:right"><span class="dir">next →</span><span class="ttl">Adoption</span></a>
        </div>
</Docs>
